# Everything here is optional — the app works with this file absent.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ matters: tiles, routing and search are fetched by the BROWSER,
# so these are inlined at build time. A server-only variable would arrive as
# undefined and silently fall back to the default.

# A different tile server, or one you host. OpenStreetMap's tile policy asks
# heavy users to run their own.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TILE_URL=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png

# Your own OSRM. The public demo server is rate limited, sometimes down, and
# reliably serves only the driving profile:
#   docker run -t -i -p 5000:5000 -v "${PWD}:/data" osrm/osrm-backend \
#     osrm-routed --algorithm mld /data/netherlands-latest.osrm
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OSRM_URL=http://localhost:5000

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Going out through this server instead of from the browser
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normally the browser fetches tiles, routing, search and Overpass itself, and
# that is the right shape — no server in the path. It stops working when a
# network in the way cannot reach those hosts at all.
#
# Point the four variables above at this app's own paths and every one of them
# goes out through the server instead. No code changes; the base URL is simply
# local:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TILE_URL=/api/osm/tile/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OSRM_URL=/api/osm/osrm
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_NOMINATIM_URL=/api/osm/nominatim
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_OVERPASS_URL=/api/osm/overpass

# ── Two proxies in a row ───────────────────────────────────────
# For a server whose own internet is filtered: one proxy is reachable, and the
# proxies that can actually get out are only reachable THROUGH it.
#
#     browser → this server → OSM_PROXY_ENTRY → one of OSM_PROXY_LIST → OSM
#
# The entry proxy is never used as the exit on its own. It is the way in to the
# second hop and nothing else.
# OSM_PROXY_ENTRY=192.168.11.165:2000

# The second hops. A comma-separated list, or leave this unset and the file
# below is read instead (default: ./proxies.json, which is committed).
# Accepts a scraper's JSON array, a comma list, or one per line.
# OSM_PROXY_LIST=
# OSM_PROXY_LIST_FILE=proxies.json

# What a probe fetches. An upstream this app actually depends on, never a
# neutral connectivity endpoint: a proxy that reaches one and not the other is
# useless here. /api/status is the cheapest thing Overpass serves.
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/status
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_INTERVAL_MS=600000
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=9000
# The entry proxy is one box on a LAN; 649 simultaneous CONNECTs would be a
# denial of service against the thing the whole chain depends on.
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_CONCURRENCY=12

# How many exits one request may try before giving up. Small on purpose — this
# is here so a blocked network can get an answer, not so more can be taken from
# Overpass than they offer. Self-hosting Overpass removes both problems.
# OSM_PROXY_ATTEMPTS=4
# OSM_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS=25000

# Try a direct connection after every proxy has failed. Off by default: on a
# filtered network it just adds a timeout to an answer that is already no.
# OSM_PROXY_ALLOW_DIRECT=false

# Both Overpass and Nominatim ask for this, and Nominatim blocks a client that
# does not send one. Going out through a rotating exit makes it MORE important:
# it is the only thing saying these requests are one small app.
# OSM_USER_AGENT=

# Where the forwarder actually goes. Comma-separated; each retry moves to the
# next one, because a rate limit is per exit IP PER HOST.
# OSM_UPSTREAM_OVERPASS=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter
# OSM_UPSTREAM_OSRM=https://router.project-osrm.org
# OSM_UPSTREAM_NOMINATIM=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# OSM_UPSTREAM_TILE=https://tile.openstreetmap.org

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The database — OPTIONAL, and unset is a working configuration
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The only thing here that needs one is the crime layer: the reports people
# enter, and the example data `npm run seed` writes.
#
# Unset, reports live in the browser that entered them and the app says so on
# the screen. That sentence matters: a report somebody believed they had filed,
# held only in their own browser and visible to nobody, is worse than not being
# able to file one at all.
#
# Keep the `/maddie` on the end. With no path the driver picks `test`, which is
# a silent write to the wrong database that looks exactly like a working one.
# MONGO_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/maddie

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The safety read
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One data source: OpenStreetMap, queried from the BROWSER like the tiles and
# the routing. Chosen over crime figures because those are published per
# neighbourhood per month — a walking route usually sits inside one
# neighbourhood, so they give every candidate route the same number and cannot
# answer "which of these two streets". OSM changes metre by metre and carries
# lit=*, which is what decides how a street feels after dark.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OVERPASS_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter

# ── The model. SERVER-SIDE, and these are NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_ ───────────────────
# The model writes the sentence. It never produces the score — that is computed
# from the map counts in src/lib/score.ts, where it can be checked. A small
# local model asked to invent a safety number would produce a confident one
# with nothing behind it.
#
# LOCAL FIRST: free per call, private (this app is told where someone is
# walking and at what hour), and it cannot be rate-limited or cut off for a
# billing failure. Unreachable from a phone, which is why this runs on the
# server rather than in the browser.
LOCAL_AI_HOST=192.168.11.165
# Blank uses http://$LOCAL_AI_HOST:1234/v1 — LM Studio's default. The literal
# value `off` disables the local tier and goes straight to the fallback.
LOCAL_AI_URL=
LOCAL_AI_MODEL=gemma-3-4b-it

# ── Liara: the fallback, for when the LAN box is off ─────────────────────────
# BOTH must be set or the tier is skipped entirely. A URL with no key 401s on
# every call, which turns "the LAN box is off" into a confusing error instead
# of a quiet degradation.
# Get these from the Liara console → AI service. Verify before relying on it:
#   curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $LIARA_AI_KEY" "$LIARA_AI_URL/models"
LIARA_AI_URL=
LIARA_AI_KEY=
LIARA_AI_MODEL=

# If neither tier answers, the page still shows the score and the findings.
# Only the sentence is lost.
